            0.741,
            0.047,
            0.862,
            0.064
          ],
          "content": "Quadrilaterals"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.085,
            0.624,
            0.108
          ],
          "content": "Now, it can be shown that ∆UXY ≅ ∆VWZ. (How?)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.111,
            0.301,
            0.13
          ],
          "content": "Thus, $\\angle \\mathbf{U} = \\angle \\mathbf{V}.$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.135,
            0.847,
            0.174
          ],
          "content": "Using this fact, the remaining angles of the isosceles trapezium can be determined. Verify the angles by measurement."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.177,
            0.847,
            0.216
          ],
          "content": "**Property 2:** In an isosceles trapezium, the angles opposite to the equal sides are equal."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.224,
            0.294,
            0.247
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Figure it Out"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.25,
            0.847,
            0.29
          ],
          "content": "1. Find all the sides and the angles of the quadrilateral obtained by joining two equilateral triangles with sides 4 cm."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.298,
            0.797,
            0.319
          ],
          "content": "2. Construct a kite whose diagonals are of lengths 6 cm and 8 cm."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.328,
            0.744,
            0.349
          ],
          "content": "3. Find the remaining angles in the following trapeziums—"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.193,
            0.376,
            0.492,
            0.501
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_25_image_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.533,
            0.349,
            0.804,
            0.517
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_25_image_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.523,
            0.848,
            0.581
          ],
          "content": "4. Draw a Venn diagram showing the set of parallelograms, kites, rhombuses, rectangles, and squares. Then, answer the following questions—"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.217,
            0.589,
            0.847,
            0.628
          ],
          "content": "(i) What is the quadrilateral that is both a kite and a parallelogram?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.21,
            0.632,
            0.847,
            0.652
          ],
          "content": "(ii) Can there be a quadrilateral that is both a kite and a rectangle?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.207,
            0.655,
            0.846,
            0.695
          ],
          "content": "(iii) Is every kite a rhombus? If not, what is the correct relationship between these two types of quadrilaterals?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.698,
            0.657,
            0.718
          ],
          "content": "5. If PAIR and RODS are two rectangles, find ∠IOD."
        },
